<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Massalia</h1><p class="st-meta">600 BCE – 49 BCE (approx.) · capital Massalia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Greek colony at Marseille, the oldest city in France, through which Greek wine, coinage and writing reached the peoples of Gaul for five centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Settlers from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/phocaea">Phocaea</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> founded the city around 600 BCE on the sheltered harbour of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lacydon">Lacydon</a>, east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> delta. Tradition says their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protis">Protis</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyptis">Gyptis</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nannus">Nannus</a>, king of the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segobrigii">Segobrigii</a>. Refugees arrived after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaea">Phocaea</a> fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 545 BCE, and Massalia planted daughter towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nikaia</a> (Nice), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antibes">Antipolis</a> (Antibes) and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emporion">Emporion</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city was an aristocratic republic governed by a council of six hundred timouchoi, praised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> for its stability. It traded wine, oil and fine pottery up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, metals and slaves, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins were widely copied by Gallic peoples. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis-of-ephesus">Artemis of Ephesus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo-delphinios">Apollo Delphinios</a> were its chief cults, and around 325 BCE its navigator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pytheas">Pytheas</a> sailed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A steady ally of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>, Massalia called in Roman armies against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/salyes">Salyes</a> from 125 BCE, opening the way to the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a>. Having sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>, it was besieged and taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a>'s forces in 49 BCE and lost its fleet and most of its territory, yet it lived on as a free Greek city under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/massalia.jpg" alt="Massalia" width="320" height="153"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Massalia_large_coin_5th_1st_century_BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 BCE – 49 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Massalia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Massilia, Marseille</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-324&amp;focus=massalia" aria-label="Massalia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4810 642 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Massalia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" 